== 1. Summary
<1-summary>
aktujo takes care of the whole life of a document: #strong[capture] it -- scanned, photographed or uploaded --, #strong[improve]
it, #strong[read] and #strong[label] it, keep every #strong[version] of it safe, #strong[find] it, #strong[share] it and run it through
#strong[workflows] -- approved by one person, signed by the next --, every step in an #strong[audit log];. Documents are end-to-end encrypted and
stay where their owners want them: on their own devices, synced directly between them, on a server of their own or
in the cloud of an organisation.

aktujo is built for every size: from an app on one computer to a cluster run by a Kubernetes operator with PostgreSQL
and S3-compatible storage -- one edition, the same software. Its core is written in Rust, secure and fast, and is a
library as well: aktujo runs as an application of its own, and other applications build it in -- in the falkOSS
family spezado (accounting), notenwart (sheet music) and kernoid (mail, identity and files).

aktujo is free software published by falkOSS: #strong[everything included] -- no paid edition, no licence keys -- and #strong[no
telemetry];.

== 2. The problem
<2-the-problem>
Paper and PDFs pile up in every household and every organisation: notices, contracts, policies, payslips, medical
letters, warranties, invoices. Handling them takes several tools -- a scanner app, a cloud folder, a document manager,
a service for signatures, e-mail for approvals -- and each of them holds a copy, while nobody can say afterwards who
saw, changed, approved or signed what. The documents are among the most personal data there is,
yet most of these tools keep them readable to whoever runs the server.

Free document managers exist, but they are servers: they need to be installed and run, they keep documents
unencrypted, and taking a picture with a phone or signing a document needs other tools (assessment, #strong[OPEN];: to be
verified against paperless-ngx and others before publication).

And every application that wants to work offline, sync between devices and encrypt end to end builds that layer
again -- in the falkOSS family alone there are three ways of storing files today.

== 3. Principles
<3-principles>
- #strong[Your documents, your choice of place.] Local-first: aktujo works on one device without any server. Syncing
  directly between one\'s own devices, over a relay or through a server is a choice, not a requirement.
- #strong[Sealed end to end.] What leaves a device is encrypted; servers and relays store and pass on what they cannot
  read.
- #strong[The original stays the original.] Every document is kept byte for byte, named by its hash; a change makes a new
  version, never overwrites. Everything aktujo reads from it is a #strong[suggestion] that a person confirms -- nothing is
  changed behind their back.
- #strong[Every step on record.] Who added, opened, changed, shared, approved or signed a document is written to an audit
  log that cannot be changed unnoticed.
- #strong[One edition for everyone.] The same software for one person and for an enterprise; every feature free.
- #strong[No telemetry.] aktujo connects only where it is needed or where its user asks it to.
- #strong[Built to be built in.] The core is a set of libraries with a stable interface, so other applications can use it
  instead of writing their own.

== 4. The life of a document
<4-the-life-of-a-document>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 2,
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Step], [What aktujo does],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Capture];], [Upload files and folders, import from a scanner or by mail; later the mobile app photographs paper directly.],
    [#strong[Improve];], [Tools for scans and photos: find the page\'s edges, straighten a page shot at an angle (perspective), crop, rotate, black and white or greyscale, contrast; several pages into one document.],
    [#strong[Read];], [The text layer of digital PDFs, OCR for scans. aktujo suggests the kind of document and its fields from rules that are data -- kinds and rules a person defines for their own documents, whether letters, sheet music, research papers or a collection of books; templates to start from; programs that build aktujo in bring the rules of their trade.],
    [#strong[Label];], [Tags, correspondents, kinds of documents and own fields; suggested from rules and from what was labelled before, confirmed by a person; rules that file new documents on their own once trusted.],
    [#strong[Version];], [Every change -- a new scan, an edited page, a signature -- is a new version beside the old ones; any version can be opened, compared and restored.],
    [#strong[Keep];], [Originals by hash and an event log of every change; encrypted on the device and on the way; retention and deletion by rule.],
    [#strong[Find];], [Full-text search, also over encrypted documents on the device that holds the key; filters by kind, sender, date and field.],
    [#strong[Share];], [With people and devices, with rights (read, add, manage); a household, a team, a tax adviser who may only read.],
    [#strong[Workflows];], [Chains of steps across people: first person A approves, then person B signs, then person C countersigns or files -- in order or side by side, with reminders, comments, rejections and a deadline per step; templates for recurring chains such as invoice approval, contracts or applications.],
    [#strong[Sign];], [Check the signatures of documents that arrive; sign as a step of a workflow or on its own -- also people without an account, by link; qualified signatures through the providers that are allowed to issue them (OPEN, see 7).],
    [#strong[Audit];], [One log per document and per workspace: who did what and when, chained by hash and signed, so a gap or a change shows; exported as a report with the document.],
    [#strong[Hand over];], [Pass a document on without copying it -- an invoice becomes a receipt in spezado, same original, same hash; export everything at any time in an open format.],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

