The data stays the same in every setting, so moving from one to the next is a sync, not a migration.

== 6. Built in, or on its own
<6-built-in-or-on-its-own>
aktujo\'s core is a set of Rust libraries: storage of originals (folder, S3, kernoid), the event log with sync,
members and keys with end-to-end encryption, the relay; the document model, recognition and the scan tools. They are
under the MIT licence, so any application may build them in -- also apps published in app stores.

- #strong[spezado] keeps its receipts in it and reads invoices with it.
- #strong[notenwart] reads sheet music with its recognition.
- #strong[kernoid] offers it to its accounts: as the place where aktujo\'s data lives in an organisation, and with
  aktujo\'s recognition for searching the files it stores.
- #strong[aktujo itself] -- command line, terminal, web app, later desktop and mobile apps -- is the application anyone can
  use on its own, without any of the others.

Recognition is written in Rust so that it runs everywhere -- on servers, on phones, in the browser -- also over
documents only the device can decrypt; established external programs (for example Tesseract) can be plugged in.

== 7. Security, audit and signatures
<7-security-audit-and-signatures>
- #strong[Encryption:] HPKE, XChaCha20-Poly1305 and Ed25519: every set of documents has its members, every member their
  devices and keys, every device what it needs.
- #strong[Audit log:] every step is an event of the log the sync is built on -- signed by the device that wrote it and
  chained by hash, so it is complete and cannot be changed unnoticed; in an organisation it can be kept longer than
  the documents it describes (OPEN: retention rules, GoBD).
- #strong[Recovery] without a server that could read along: a recovery code, a second device, or -- in an organisation --
  a recovery the organisation holds, if it chooses so.
- #strong[Signatures] in three steps: first checking the signatures of incoming documents (PAdES); then signing within
  workflows -- simple and advanced electronic signatures under eIDAS, with identities from an OpenID Connect provider such
  as kernoid; then qualified signatures through those who are allowed to issue them: qualified trust service
  providers and the European Digital Identity Wallet. aktujo does not become a trust service provider itself.
  #strong[OPEN:] the legal requirements per kind of document, the state of the German wallet, Rust libraries for PAdES.

== 8. Licences and governance
<8-licences-and-governance>
aktujo is free software published by falkOSS -- everything included, no telemetry. The program is under
AGPL-3.0-or-later; the libraries other projects build on and the apps for the app stores are under MIT; this text is
under CC BY 4.0.

== 9. The name
<9-the-name>
```
akto   a document that attests something
 -ujo  a container for it
      → aktujo
```

#emph[aktujo] is Esperanto for a document folder. #emph[akto] is an official document that attests something -- a notice, a
contract, a certificate -- and the suffix #emph[-ujo] makes a container for it. The Reta Vortaro, one of the standard
dictionaries, describes an #emph[aktujo] as a container for documents, at its simplest a folded sheet of cardboard \[1\].
Said ak-TU-jo, stressed on the second-to-last syllable like every Esperanto word.

== 10. Roadmap
<10-roadmap>
+ The core: storage of originals, event log, encryption, local storage, relay.
+ Recognition in Rust, measured against Tesseract; the scan tools.
+ aktujo 0.1: one person, one computer -- capture, improve, read, label, versions, keep (encrypted, in several
  spaces), find; also as aktujo-minimal, without scanning and OCR. Import from other programs comes later (OPEN:
  when).
+ Sync between own devices and sharing; the audit log; the mobile app with the camera.
+ Server and organisations: Docker Compose for one server; PostgreSQL, S3 and the Kubernetes operator for high
  availability; kernoid as login and storage.
+ Workflows and signatures: checking signatures, approval chains, signing, then qualified signatures.

